{
  "schema": 1,
  "grade": "6",
  "band": "6-8",
  "focus": "Grade 6 moves students from knowing a word to using it precisely. The year builds the vocabulary of character, evidence, cause and effect, comparison, argument and discussion, plus the first systematic work with Latin and Greek word parts (the negating prefix in- and its disguises, pend/pens, mit/miss, struct, vis/vid and aud) and the Tier 3 language of math, science and story analysis. Compared with grade 5 it asks for shades of meaning among near-synonyms and for words that carry an argument; compared with grade 7 it keeps concepts concrete and personal (people, events, everyday systems) before tone, claims about sources, and data enter at full strength.",
  "selection": "Each word was chosen because a sixth grader has met it in grade-level text and can half-define it but cannot yet use it precisely (Tier 2), or because it is a specific term that unlocks a content class (Tier 3, never more than three per disciplinary unit). Selection criteria: academic utility across subjects, comprehension value in literary and informational text, semantic nuance within a field, morphological productivity (real Latin roots and affixes only), argument and discussion language, confusable pairs, multilingual-learner value through Spanish cognates, and a defensible grade-to-grade difficulty ramp. Roots already taught in grades 4 and 5 (port, rupt, spect, dict, scrib/script, bio, graph) and the suffix and prefix families of those grades were not repeated. Tract, vert/vers, ced/cess, fract/sect, cred, bene/mal and chron, and the tone, bias, ethics and rhetoric vocabularies, were deliberately left to grades 7 and 8. The generic task-verb set (justify, interpret, analyze, evaluate) is owned by grades 3 to 5 and 7, so grade 6's disciplinary strand covers social studies, math and earth science instead. Sets cohere by theme, function, root or semantic field; no existing publisher list or sequence was consulted.",
  "arc": [
    "Reading People — Character and behavior",
    "Evidence in Plain Sight — Observation and evidence: noticing what is really there",
    "Not, in Four Disguises — The negating prefix in- and its assimilated forms il-, im-, ir-",
    "Ancient Civilizations — Social studies: how early societies rose, ruled and fell",
    "Becoming Something New — Change and transformation: gradual, sudden and cyclical",
    "Making a Case — Argument and persuasion: what a claim needs",
    "Hanging and Weighing — The root pend/pens: hanging, weighing and paying out",
    "Because of That — Cause and effect: triggers, influence and result words",
    "Math Talk: Numbers in Relationship — Mathematics: ratios, integers and quantity",
    "Inside a Story — Literary analysis: how plots are built and why characters act",
    "Send It Out — The roots mit and miss: sending and letting go",
    "Weighing Your Options — Decision-making and problem-solving",
    "Alike and Different — Comparison and contrast: words and connectors",
    "Earth in Motion — Earth science: the slow forces that shape land",
    "Under Construction — The root struct: building, arranging and tearing down",
    "Neighbors and Norms — Communities and society: what groups share and expect",
    "Talking It Over — Discussion and communication: building on what others say",
    "Telling True Stories — Nonfiction narrative: memoir, anecdote and first-hand accounts",
    "Eyes and Ears — The roots vis/vid and aud: seeing and hearing",
    "The Right Word — Precision and nuance: choosing among close words"
  ],
